// Bytes which no rule matches. Every failed attempt consumes the bytes the automaton looked at including the one it
// could not consume, so two unmatchable bytes next to each other are two errors and not one, and the scan carries on
// afterwards instead of giving up. A single bad byte therefore never hides the rest of the input.
//
// The parser is not what this case is about, so the grammar is the single empty production the file format requires.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    DIGITS: /[0-9]+/;
}

@parser {
    tokens
        : @empty
        ;
}
